{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And it was on the third day.</b> It was the day of Shabbat, and the giving of the Torah was on it, and it was the third day of that day, which is the fourth of the month, and the fifth day of the week was Shabbat. Here is the explanation for you of the six days, from the first of the month of Sivan until the sixth of it, on which the Torah was given, according to the order of the verses, according to the Sages who hold that it was given on the sixth of Sivan. And now I shall explain to you the position of Rabbi Yosi, who holds that the Torah was given on the seventh of Sivan. 'On this day' — on the first of the month of Sivan, which was on the first day of the week, on Shabbat, for it is established for us that the first of Nisan, on which they went out of Egypt, was the fifth day of the week; if so, the first of Sivan adjacent to it fell on the first day of the week, on Shabbat. On that very day they came to the wilderness of Sinai, and on that day He did not say anything to them because of the fatigue of the journey. And Moses went up on the second day, 'and He called to him,' etc., in order, until 'all that HaShem has spoken we will do.' The next day, on the third day, 'Moses set down the words of the people,' who had said, 'all that HaShem has spoken we will do.' And on that very day it was said, 'Behold, I come to you,' etc., until 'that they may believe forever.' The next day, on the fourth day, 'Moses told the words of the people,' etc. And on that very day it was said, 'and to Moses He said,' etc. — this interprets the statement above, 'and you shall sanctify yourselves today and tomorrow' — the fourth day and the fifth day, 'and they shall be ready for the third day.' And 'the third day' of that (fourth day) will be the seventh of the month, the day of Shabbat, on which the Torah was given according to everyone. For on that third day He descended, etc., in order, until 'they went up on the mountain and Moses descended.' On that very day, 'and he said to the people.' On that fourth day they prepared for three days, which are six periods of daylight — from dawn of the fourth day, during which they stood, until dawn of Shabbat — for Moses added of his own understanding one more day of separation beyond what the Holy One, blessed be He, had commanded him. For the Holy One, blessed be He, said 'today and tomorrow,' which implies the fourth day and the fifth day, and he added the sixth day. And according to the one who says לשלשת יתירה — it is extra, like the one who says לכל חיל פרעה, לכל כליו תעשה נחשת. That is, they were prepared and separated three full days, which are six periods. As Rashi explained. And what Rashi explained, לשלשת ימים — at the end of three days — that is the fourth day: this is Shabbat, which enters at the end of the third day into the fourth day of the week, and it is the fourth (day) counted from the fourth day of the week on which Moses was standing." "<b>At the drawing of the dawn.</b> Rashi explains: this teaches that He preceded them, etc. — that is, on their account, for their sake." "<b>And all the people trembled.</b> This is not an expression of fear but an expression of noise and movement, like 'והמצב חרדו' and 'כל העם חרדו אחריו.' That is, they heard the sounds and were still upon their beds, and they sensed that the Shekhinah was waiting for them upon the mountain; therefore they trembled and moved."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The text uses a counting scheme where days of the month and days of the week share ordinal names; the parenthetical clarifications attempt to track the overlapping counts. 'Six periods' (שש עונות) refers to three days counted as day-periods and night-periods. The Rashbam reference is spelled פרש״י as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:01.628739331Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}